{
  "esm": "2.3.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "ppmflux_D_lev_hyb_nf_bottom",
    "description": "The terrain-following (hybrid sigma-pressure), mass-form, upwind-biased monotone PPM vertical flux difference F_{k+1/2} - F_{k-1/2} over the three levels next to the ground (k = 1..3) k = 1..NLEV of every column at once. Each face flux is ../stencils/ppmflux_lev_face_flux (F = max(W,0)*aR_L + min(W,0)*aL_R) on the six-cell window of the tracer q and of the physical pressure thicknesses dp (free name, shape [lon,lat,lev]): the upper face k+1/2 reads cells k-2..k+3 and W[i,j,k+1], the lower face k-1/2 reads cells k-3..k+2 and W[i,j,k]. Near the rigid walls the window reaches past the column; the out-of-column members are EDGE-EXTENDED, read at the nearest in-column cell (max(k-3,1), ..., min(k+3,NLEV)) -- exactly the near-wall reconstruction the no-flux rule has always used. The wall fluxes are zero: the upper face of cell NLEV and the lower face of cell 1 are the model top and the ground, so each face term is ifelse(the face is interior, F, 0). Cell 1 is therefore F_{3/2} - 0 and cell NLEV is 0 - F_{NLEV-1/2}, bit for bit the one-sided wall differences. Writing the edge extension as clamped subscripts and the walls as a select keeps the level an ordinary array axis, so a whole three-level slab is one aggregate instead of three one-level stencils. MASS FORM: no division by a cell thickness (the consumer integrates the extensive cell mass; conservative while the layers breathe). At q == 1 every slope is a difference of equal values, exactly 0.0, so F = W bitwise and this is the mass-form continuity difference. Only the ground's closure can act on these levels (requires NLEV >= 6), so only that side's reads carry it; the window members toward the other wall are read by plain offsets. The interior levels use ./ppmflux_D_lev_hyb_interior and the opposite slab ./ppmflux_D_lev_hyb_nf_top.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:stencil",
      "family:finite_volume",
      "grid:latlon3d",
      "op:D",
      "axes:lon,lat,lev",
      "order:3",
      "scheme:upwind",
      "stencil_width:7"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Colella, P., & Woodward, P. R. (1984). The Piecewise Parabolic Method (PPM) for gas-dynamical simulations. Journal of Computational Physics, 54(1), 174-201.",
        "doi": "10.1016/0021-9991(84)90143-8"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Lin, S.-J., & Rood, R. B. (1996). Multidimensional flux-form semi-Lagrangian transport schemes. Monthly Weather Review, 124(9), 2046-2070.",
        "doi": "10.1175/1520-0493(1996)124<2046:MFFSLT>2.0.CO;2"
      }
    ]
  },
